WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.826
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.126 --> 00:00:09.687
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.987 --> 00:00:16.853
Unit two: International Organizations. Today you will combine ideas efficiently with participle clauses.

4
00:00:17.153 --> 00:00:19.829
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:24.162
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:26.412
United Nations.

7
00:00:27.417 --> 00:00:32.388
United Nations means the world organisation of countries that works for peace and cooperation.

8
00:00:32.983 --> 00:00:36.840
The United Nations was founded in 1945.

9
00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:40.218
Your turn. Say: United Nations.

10
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:44.509
United Nations.

11
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:46.448
Multilateral.

12
00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:50.448
Multilateral means involving three or more countries.

13
00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:53.758
A multilateral agreement needs many signatures.

14
00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.142
Your turn. Say: Multilateral.

15
00:00:60.000 --> 00:01:01.268
Multilateral.

16
00:01:02.017 --> 00:01:03.215
Member state.

17
00:01:04.217 --> 00:01:08.445
Member state means a country that belongs to an international organisation.

18
00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:10.922
Every member state has one vote.

19
00:01:11.517 --> 00:01:14.308
Your turn. Say: Member state.

20
00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:18.388
Member state.

21
00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:20.174
Peacekeeping.

22
00:01:21.183 --> 00:01:25.412
Peacekeeping means the use of international forces to keep fighting from restarting.

23
00:01:26.017 --> 00:01:28.469
Peacekeeping reduced violence in the region.

24
00:01:29.083 --> 00:01:31.782
Your turn. Say: Peacekeeping.

25
00:01:34.633 --> 00:01:35.681
Peacekeeping.

26
00:01:36.417 --> 00:01:38.347
International cooperation.

27
00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:44.031
International cooperation means countries working together to reach shared goals.

28
00:01:44.617 --> 00:01:47.673
International cooperation ended the epidemic.

29
00:01:48.283 --> 00:01:52.027
Your turn. Say: International cooperation.

30
00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:56.936
International cooperation.

31
00:01:57.683 --> 00:01:58.742
Resolution.

32
00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:03.758
Resolution means a formal decision made by a group after a vote.

33
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:06.965
The resolution passed by a large majority.

34
00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:10.539
Your turn. Say: Resolution.

35
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.564
Resolution.

36
00:02:15.317 --> 00:02:16.875
Humanitarian aid.

37
00:02:17.883 --> 00:02:22.658
Humanitarian aid means food, medicine and help sent to people in crisis.

38
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.900
Humanitarian aid reached the flooded towns.

39
00:02:26.483 --> 00:02:29.623
Your turn. Say: Humanitarian aid.

40
00:02:32.467 --> 00:02:34.164
Humanitarian aid.

41
00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:36.312
Global governance.

42
00:02:37.317 --> 00:02:41.708
Global governance means the ways countries manage problems that affect the whole world.

43
00:02:42.317 --> 00:02:45.338
Global governance needs fair and effective rules.

44
00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:48.927
Your turn. Say: Global governance.

45
00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:53.151
Global governance.

46
00:02:53.967 --> 00:02:57.397
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:04.409
Visal, a report says 'Having signed the charter, member states must follow its rules.' What is 'Having signed'?

48
00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:17.457
A perfect participle clause. It shows one action finished before another, and both share one subject: Having signed the charter, the states must follow it.

49
00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:19.865
And without 'having'?

50
00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:36.376
The present participle shows simultaneous actions: 'Working together, nations deliver aid faster.' The past participle is passive: 'Adopted in 1948, the declaration remains influential.'

51
00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:38.786
Is there any warning?

52
00:03:39.883 --> 00:03:52.711
Yes, the subject must match. Not 'Having voted, the resolution passed', because a resolution cannot vote. Say 'Having voted, the members passed the resolution.'

53
00:03:53.867 --> 00:03:56.006
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:02.890
Grammar focus: Participle clauses: Having plus past participle and -ing and -ed.

55
00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:04.423
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:05.117 --> 00:04:08.730
Having signed the charter, member states must follow its rules.

57
00:04:09.617 --> 00:04:13.555
Working together, nations deliver humanitarian aid faster.

58
00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:19.642
Adopted in 1948, the declaration remains influential today.

59
00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:30.358
A participle clause shares the subject of the main clause. Use Having + past participle for an earlier action, -ing for a simultaneous one, -ed for a passive one.

60
00:04:31.117 --> 00:04:35.545
Be careful! Don't say: Having voted, the resolution passed.

61
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:40.695
Say: Having voted, the members passed it.

62
00:04:40.995 --> 00:04:43.552
Both clauses must share the same subject.

63
00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:47.089
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:49.911
Working Through the United Nations.

65
00:04:50.211 --> 00:04:55.403
The United Nations brings countries together to solve problems that no single nation can manage.

66
00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.975
Having joined, each member state agrees to respect the charter and to support international cooperation.

67
00:05:02.967 --> 00:05:07.648
Acting through a resolution, the Security Council can authorise peacekeeping missions.

68
00:05:08.633 --> 00:05:13.013
Funded by member states, humanitarian aid reaches millions of people every year.

69
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:19.866
Critics argue that global governance would work better if every multilateral decision were taken more quickly.

70
00:05:20.967 --> 00:05:24.864
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:25.717 --> 00:05:28.575
blank the report, the delegates left the hall.

72
00:05:28.875 --> 00:05:33.295
A, Having read. B, Have read. C, To having read.

73
00:05:36.833 --> 00:05:38.984
The answer is A: Having read.

74
00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:43.438
An agreement involving many countries is described as blank.

75
00:05:43.738 --> 00:05:47.798
A, bilateral. B, multilateral. C, private.

76
00:05:51.333 --> 00:05:53.890
The answer is B: multilateral.

77
00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:57.781
Which sentence has a correct participle clause?

78
00:05:58.081 --> 00:06:06.227
A, Signing the treaty, the vote was held.. B, Having signed the treaty, the leaders went home.. C, Signed the treaty, the leaders went home..

79
00:06:09.767 --> 00:06:13.496
The answer is B: Having signed the treaty, the leaders went home..

80
00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:18.670
Food, medicine and help sent to people in crisis is humanitarian blank.

81
00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:21.718
A, aid. B, debt. C, trade.

82
00:06:25.267 --> 00:06:27.289
The answer is A: aid.

83
00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:32.063
According to the reading, what can the Security Council authorise?

84
00:06:32.363 --> 00:06:37.422
A, tariffs. B, peacekeeping missions. C, elections.

85
00:06:40.967 --> 00:06:43.930
The answer is B: peacekeeping missions.

86
00:06:45.133 --> 00:06:48.099
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:52.668
Why might countries choose to work through international organizations?

88
00:07:01.483 --> 00:07:02.856
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:03.156 --> 00:07:28.253
Countries choose international organizations because many problems, such as pandemics, climate change and conflict, cross borders. Working through a multilateral body lets states share costs, set common rules and speak with greater authority. It also gives smaller countries a voice and offers a neutral place to settle disputes peacefully.

90
00:07:28.553 --> 00:07:30.355
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:38.128
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and participle clauses: having plus past participle and -ing and -ed.

92
00:07:38.428 --> 00:07:43.275
Today's value is global cooperation. Shared problems need shared solutions.

93
00:07:43.575 --> 00:07:46.192
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

94
00:07:46.492 --> 00:07:49.354
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

